C = [
("h1", "13. Objections and Replies"),
("p", "This chapter takes the strongest objections to the position and answers each from the text. The objections are stated as their holders state them, without caricature. Where a view is held by a tradition, the tradition is named, and no individual is credited with a statement the volume has not read."),
("sub", "13.1 “The church is the bride of Christ”"),
("p", "This is the most widely held reading, and it has been held for many centuries. Hymns, sermons and many confessions speak of the church as the bride of Christ, and those who hold it point to Ephesians 5, to 2 Corinthians 11:2 and to Revelation 19 and 21. The reading expresses things that are true: Christ loves the church, gave himself for it, and will present it holy. Those truths are not in dispute. A modern example is Thomas Constable’s //Expository Notes// (2012), which says on Revelation 19:7, “The bride of the Lamb is evidently the church.” He gives a reason for excluding Israel: “Israel cannot be this bride or part of this bride because this bride comes to earth with Christ, but Old Testament saints will not experience resurrection until Christ returns to the earth.” The volume answers him from his own notes on chapter 21. Of the gates he writes, “The fact that each gate-tower bears a name of one of Israel’s tribes probably indicates that Israel will have a distinctive identity and role in this city.” The chapter itself says nothing of the order of the resurrections, and it shows the wife as the city whose gates carry Israel’s names. The volume holds that the text of Revelation 19 and 21 should decide who the wife is, and the order of events should be read after it. The same reading is held by a long line of dispensational teachers, among them Robert Thomas in his commentary on Revelation 8 to 22, Charles Ryrie in //Basic Theology// and J. Dwight Pentecost in //Things to Come//. The volume describes the position of Thomas, Ryrie and Pentecost in its own words and does not quote them. John MacArthur is quoted below from the free transcript of his sermon on the passage."),
("p", "John MacArthur’s sermon on Revelation 19 holds that the bride is the church, and it says of the city of Revelation 21, “The bride is the church that occupies the city.” The same sermon then adds, “The bride is distinctively the church, but it is more than the church,” and goes on to place resurrected Old Testament saints, tribulation saints and kingdom saints in the final form of the bride. The reading that begins by making the wife the church is thus widened by its own teacher to take in Israel’s saints when the city appears. The volume asks why the widening is needed. If the wife of Revelation 21 includes the saints of Israel, and the gates bear Israel’s names, the plain reading is that she is Israel’s wife, as she is in the prophets."),
("p", "John Walvoord’s //The Revelation of Jesus Christ// states the church-bride reading with care, and some of its sentences bear on the question here. Walvoord grants the prophets’ usage: “Israel is already married to Jehovah,” and has proved unfaithful. He finds the New Testament figure different: “the church is pictured as a virgin waiting for the coming of her bridegroom.” On Revelation 19 he writes that “the wife of the Lamb is distinguished from the attendants at the wedding, the wife apparently being the church.” Three points follow. The first is that he agrees with this volume that the wedding has two parties, a wife and attendants. The second is that he agrees that in the Old Testament the wife of the LORD is Israel. The third is that his identification of the wife of Revelation 19 as the church is, in his own word, “apparently” drawn, and it rests on a distinction between ages that, in his words, “seems to be observed in this passage.” The chapter itself does not state that distinction. The word the chapter uses is wife, which is the word the prophets use. If that word names Israel in Hosea and Isaiah, the reader needs a reason in Revelation to say that it names another people, and the chapter gives none."),
("p", "The reply has four points. The first is wording: the phrase “the Bride of Christ” does not stand in the King James Version, and where the words bride and wife are used of the Lamb, the chapters that use them name the wife by her city and her tribes (Revelation 21:9–14). The second is the prophets, who gave the title long before and gave it to Israel (Chapters 1 to 4). The third is Paul’s own figures for the Body, which are the body, the temple and the household, with marriage used as a comparison (Chapters 10 and 11). The fourth is the principle of no transfer. A title that the prophets gave to Israel is not moved to the Body because the Body is loved by the same Lord."),
("sub", "13.2 “Ephesians 5:32 calls it a great mystery, so the church must be the bride”"),
("p", "The objection is that Paul quotes the law of marriage and says, “This is a great mystery: but I speak concerning Christ and the church” (Ephesians 5:32), and that he could not speak so if the church were not the bride. The reply is in the way the passage is built. The word that joins the two loves is “even as,” and the church is called a church, a body and an “it,” never a bride. What Paul presents is “a glorious church” (Ephesians 5:27). The mystery that he calls great is the union of one flesh, and the union of Christ and the church in this letter is the union of a head and its body (Ephesians 1:22–23). The prophets’ wife is not mentioned in the passage, and the passage gives no instruction that depends on her."),
("sub", "13.3 “Paul says he espoused the Corinthians to one husband”"),
("p", "The objection is that if a pastor can say that he has espoused a congregation to Christ, the congregation must be the bride. The church-bride reading adds that the verse presents the church as a virgin awaiting her bridegroom, and Thomas Constable’s //Expository Notes// say on the verse, “Paul pictured himself as the father of a virgin bride.” That is the point the reply makes. The verse says what Paul has done and why: “I have espoused you to one husband, that I may present you as a chaste virgin to Christ” (2 Corinthians 11:2). The comparison is introduced by “as,” and its purpose is stated in the next verse, which is a fear that their minds be corrupted from the simplicity that is in Christ (2 Corinthians 11:3). The figure is pastoral. If the verse made the Corinthians the bride, it would make Paul, who did the espousing, the father of the bride, and it would require the verse to be read beside Revelation 19, which it does not mention."),
("sub", "13.4 “Israel was put away and the church took her place”"),
("p", "This is the replacement view, and it is held by a whole tradition that reads the church as the new Israel. Its strength is that the prophets did charge Israel with adultery and did speak of a putting away. The reply is that the same prophets name no other wife. They call the same children to return (Jeremiah 3:14), say that the forsaking is for “a small moment” and that the gathering is with great mercies (Isaiah 54:7), promise an everlasting covenant to the same woman (Ezekiel 16:60), and describe the return of “the children of Israel” to the LORD and David their king in the latter days (Hosea 3:5)."),
("p", "Paul asks the question himself and answers it: “Hath God cast away his people? God forbid” (Romans 11:1). He adds, “God hath not cast away his people which he foreknew” (Romans 11:2), and at the end of the argument, “the gifts and calling of God are without repentance” (Romans 11:29). Four boundaries govern the reading of Romans 11 here. The olive tree is prophetic blessing and is not the Body of Christ. The Gentiles of the chapter are participants in that blessing and are not thereby members of the Body. The wild branches are not members of the Body. Grafting changes participation and does not change identity. Participation is not identity. Blessing is not covenant. Standing is not program membership. The chapter’s teaching that Israel has not been cast away does not make the church Israel, and it does not make the church the wife."),
("sub", "13.5 “Galatians 4 quotes Isaiah 54:1 and calls the Jerusalem above the mother of us all”"),
("p", "This is the objection of most weight from Paul, and it should be heard in full. Paul quotes Isaiah’s verse, “Rejoice, thou barren that bearest not; break forth and cry, thou that travailest not: for the desolate hath many more children than she which hath an husband” (Galatians 4:27), and he says, “Jerusalem which is above is free, which is the mother of us all” (Galatians 4:26). If the desolate woman of Isaiah 54 is the mother of the believers, the objection runs, then the wife of Isaiah 54 and Isaiah 62 is the mother of the church, and the church is her family."),
("p", "The reply begins with Paul’s own label: “Which things are an allegory” (Galatians 4:24). He is setting two women and two covenants side by side, one from Mount Sinai and one that is free, and the point of the figure is the contrast between bondage and freedom (Galatians 4:24–26, 30–31). The woman he names is a mother. She is called “the mother of us all,” and the believers are her children. He does not call her the wife of the Lamb or the bride, and he does not say that the believers are the wife. In the verse itself the woman “which hath an husband” is the one the desolate woman outnumbers, and Paul draws no line from either woman to the wife of the Lamb. The Jerusalem above is “above,” while the bride of Revelation is “coming down from God out of heaven.” The shared words call for a comparison of the passages, and no transfer of the title follows from them. The city and its dwellers are the subject of the volume in this series on the eschatological community."),
("sub", "13.6 “Hosea 2:23 is quoted for the Gentiles in Romans 9”"),
("p", "Paul does take words from Hosea for those “whom he hath called, not of the Jews only, but also of the Gentiles” (Romans 9:24): “I will call them my people, which were not my people; and her beloved, which was not beloved” (Romans 9:25). The objection is that the wife’s words have moved to the church. The reply is in what Paul quotes. The clauses are the clauses of the name-reversal, about a people and a beloved who were not so called. The clauses of the betrothal (Hosea 2:19–20) and of the marriage (Hosea 2:16) are not quoted. Paul’s purpose in the chapter is the call, and the use of the words for the call does not move the title of the wife."),
("sub", "13.7 “Hebrews 12 says believers have come to the heavenly Jerusalem”"),
("p", "The objection quotes the words, “ye are come unto mount Sion, and unto the city of the living God, the heavenly Jerusalem” (Hebrews 12:22), and holds that the city is the bride of Revelation 21, so that believers belong to the bride’s city. The reply has three points. The verbs are about coming to a place, among a list of things come to, and none of them is a statement about a wife. The letter is built on the covenant it quotes, which is made “with the house of Israel and with the house of Judah” (Hebrews 8:8), and it speaks of the people to whom that covenant is made (Hebrews 8:10). And the whole question of who dwells in the city belongs to the volume on the eschatological community, where the passage receives its full reading."),
("sub", "13.8 “The Song of Solomon is the song of Christ and the church”"),
("p", "Many readers have taken the Song as a picture of Christ and the church, and some use it to show the church as bride. The reply is that the Song names neither, and that no New Testament writer quotes it to describe the Lord and the church. The volume does not need to reject a devotional use of the Song, and it holds that a doctrine of identity must be built on passages that state the identity."),
("sub", "13.9 “If the Body is not the bride, it has no close union with Christ”"),
("p", "The objection fears that to say the Body is not the bride is to say that the Body is less near to Christ. The reply is in Paul’s words. The Body is “members of his body, of his flesh, and of his bones” (Ephesians 5:30), the church “which is his body, the fulness of him that filleth all in all” (Ephesians 1:23), and it is in Christ in the heavenly places (Ephesians 2:6). It is hard to name a nearer union than that of a body with its head. The position does not reduce the Body’s nearness. It keeps the Body’s own name."),
("call", "Where the argument stands", "The objections draw their strength from real truths about Christ’s love for the church and from words that Paul shares with the prophets. The replies rest on what the passages say: the church is never called the bride, the prophets name no other wife, and a shared word is a reason to compare the passages and not to move the title."),
]
